an as a call to action to save the Odzala-Kokoua National Park in the heart of the Congo Basin, which is the second-largest tropical forest in the world after the Amazon, from the threats of climate change. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/15/arts/15congotales-1/merlin_146798859_5b0cba4d-7982-4fd9-845d-8cd0f8e03bc7-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/12/15/arts/15congotales-1/merlin_146798859_5b0cba4d-7982-4fd9-845d-8cd0f8e03bc7-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 450}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/15/arts/15congotales-1/merlin_146798859_5b0cba4d-7982-4fd9-845d-8cd0f8e03bc7-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/15/arts/15congotales-1/merlin_146798859_5b0cba4d-7982-4fd9-845d-8cd0f8e03bc7-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1536, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/15/arts/15congotales-1/15congotales-1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/12/15/arts/15congotales-1/15congotales-1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/15/arts/15congotales-1/15congotales-1-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The People of Mbomo Tell Their Stories', 'kicker': 'Footnotes', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The People of Mbomo Tell Their Tales', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Congo Tales: Told by the People of Mbomo (Book)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Congo, Democratic Republic of (Congo-Kinshasa)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-12-01T10:00:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lovia Gyarkye and Pieter Henket', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lovia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gyarkye', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Pieter', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Henket', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a03a9f34-8052-5a1c-8862-e259c4db062b', 'word_count': 855, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a03a9f34-8052-5a1c-8862-e259c4db062b'}, {'abstract': 'Covering the tragic escalation of an epidemic ', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/12/01/health/aids-day-photography-1980s.html', 'snippet': 'Covering the tragic escalation of an epidemic ', 'lead_paragraph': 'When the World Health Organization declared Dec. 1 World AIDS Day in 1988, the disease was already a global pandemic. By the end of that year, 82,362 cases of AIDS had been reported in the United States, and more than 61,000 people had died nationwide. In the 30 years since, the disease has killed an estimated 35.4 million people in all, including more than 700,000 in the United States. Today, there are still some 36.9 million people living with H.I.V. and AIDS around the world. The war is far from over in the United States. If current trends continue, half of all black gay and bisexual men will be living with HIV during their lifetimes. The epidemic is hitting hardest in the South, the region of our country with the fewest resources to combat it.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Loss and Bravery: Intimate Snapshots From the First Decade of the AIDS Crisis', 'kicker': 'Past Tense', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Loss, Bravery and a Deadly Disease', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Acquired Immune Deficiency Syndrome', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Homosexuality and Bisexuality', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Times', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-12-01T10:00:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/73cca017-0a00-5013-87ac-2b923ad5ed49', 'word_count': 864, 'uri': 'nyt://article/73cca017-0a00-5013-87ac-2b923ad5ed49'}, {'abstract': 'In Nigeria’s largest city, boundary-breaking style pushes up against tradition and social conservatism.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/12/01/style/nigeria-lagos-fashion-experimental.html', 'snippet': 'In Nigeria’s largest city, boundary-breaking style pushes up against tradition and social conservatism.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LAGOS, Nigeria — Charly Boy decided he would wear rouge. It was the 1970s, the height of the sexual revolution in the West, so it wasn’t unusual for men to be seen in makeup. ', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/02/fashion/02THELOOK1/02THELOOK1-ar