t', 'value': 'Youth', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-12-04T10:00:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Well', 'section_name': 'Well', 'subsection_name': 'Family', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julie Halpert', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Halpert', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/46aa2ebf-5d0b-5f41-a93d-26499f88bd99', 'word_count': 1385, 'uri': 'nyt://article/46aa2ebf-5d0b-5f41-a93d-26499f88bd99'}, {'abstract': 'Con artistry can charm us as much as it enrages us. And lately, it seems ubiquitous.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/12/04/magazine/why-are-we-suddenly-surrounded-by-grift.html', 'snippet': 'Con artistry can charm us as much as it enrages us. And lately, it seems ubiquitous.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The two most jarring events in the past decade of American life both had the whiff of a grand con about them. One was the crisis of 2008, after which a lot of ordinary Americans turned their attention to the financial-services industry and discovered something that looked, on the surface, uncannily like a classic bilking: There was a lot of hard-to-follow shifting about of who owned what and who owed what to whom, and in the end a lot of people found that their retirement savings had vanished. The other was the election of Donald Trump, who has always been fairly open about his talent for old-school hucksterism. To the very end, many people were convinced his entire campaign had been a long-game self-promotional exercise — a man wandering past real estate, casinos, reality television, mail-order steaks and wealth-building seminars to arrive at right-wing politics as a high-quality grift.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/09/magazine/09mag-FirstWords-1/09mag-FirstWords-1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/12/09/magazine/09mag-FirstWords-1/09mag-FirstWords-1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/09/magazine/09mag-FirstWords-1/09mag-FirstWords-1-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/09/magazine/09mag-FirstWords-1/09mag-FirstWords-1-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/09/magazine/09mag-FirstWords-1/09mag-FirstWords-1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/12/09/magazine/09mag-FirstWords-1/09mag-FirstWords-1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/09/magazine/09mag-FirstWords-1/09mag-FirstWords-1-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Why Are We Suddenly Surrounded by ‘Grift’?', 'kicker': 'First Words', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Fair Game', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Frauds and Swindling', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'na