, 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Caramanica', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e516d4f3-e6ea-5f9d-a319-ba114d503c0c', 'word_count': 954, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e516d4f3-e6ea-5f9d-a319-ba114d503c0c'}, {'abstract': 'Genre lines continued to blur and the definition of album shifted further in another year of upheaval in pop music. ', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/12/06/arts/music/best-albums.html', 'snippet': 'Genre lines continued to blur and the definition of album shifted further in another year of upheaval in pop music. ', 'lead_paragraph': 'The interaction of human and machine has been a major theme of Janelle Monáe’s entire recording career. Her latest concept album, “Dirty Computer,” deploys funky riffs (often with Prince echoes), snappy beats and crisp pop-song forms to promise that love, polymorphous sensuality and an inclusive American spirit can conquer all, even an impending apocalypse. Meanwhile, Monáe’s full-length accompanying video — billed as an “emotion picture” — is far more dystopian. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/09/arts/09BESTOFPOPALBUM2018/09BESTOFPOPALBUM2018-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/12/09/arts/09BESTOFPOPALBUM2018/09BESTOFPOPALBUM2018-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/09/arts/09BESTOFPOPALBUM2018/09BESTOFPOPALBUM2018-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'cap