ilm that touches on themes of individuality, bravery and acceptance. It profiles Laurin as she grieves the loss of her husband and learns how to let their young son, Danny, be who he is.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Film Club: ‘A Mother’s Promise: You Can Be Yourself’', 'kicker': 'Film Club', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2018-12-06T17:00:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Learning', 'section_name': 'The Learning Network', 'byline': {'original': 'By Natalie Proulx', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Natalie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Proulx', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d3712461-c31b-52ec-a543-fbfd58f1fec1', 'word_count': 422, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d3712461-c31b-52ec-a543-fbfd58f1fec1'}, {'abstract': 'When running for safety, aphid nymphs crawl onto the backs of their elders. The adult aphids don’t seem to like it. ', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/12/06/science/aphid-nymphs-hitchhiking.html', 'snippet': 'When running for safety, aphid nymphs crawl onto the backs of their elders. The adult aphids don’t seem to like it. ', 'lead_paragraph': 'A spring-green aphid clambers over a clot of soil, busily making its way to the shelter of a forest of plants in the distance. The insect’s long legs help it lever itself over the uneven ground at surprising speed, but if you look closely at its back, you’ll see that it has a passenger: A tiny juvenile aphid, or nymph, is riding the adult cowboy-style.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/07/science/07TB-APHIDS/merlin_147804054_6d4a0a82-8fae-4761-846d-dd600fc66387-articleLarge.jpg', 'heigh