lename': None, 'lastname': 'Ford', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Wendy', 'middlename': 'S.', 'lastname': 'Cutler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/ee54eb74-bbd6-5afd-b2aa-b2d1df1ae83b', 'word_count': 854, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ee54eb74-bbd6-5afd-b2aa-b2d1df1ae83b'}, {'abstract': 'All martial arts are a quest for beauty and transcendence. I found those not in doing karate but rather having karate done to me.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/12/08/opinion/karate-martial-arts-tao-gravity-.html', 'snippet': 'All martial arts are a quest for beauty and transcendence. I found those not in doing karate but rather having karate done to me.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Two hundred men and women knelt in rapt and reverential silence. All of them wore crisp, white karate outfits, or gis, cinched with black belts. Hardened karate-ka who had trained two decades and earned third- and fourth-dan black belts took honored places in the front row. I was in the back by the fire exit. We were gathered in a university gym in Toronto for an international tournament. I was in my late 30s at the time. With my still new first dan, I felt like a humble 10-handicapper in the company of Tiger Woods.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/08/opinion/08sporting/08sporting-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 343, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/12/08/opinion/08sporting/08sporting-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 343}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/08/opinion/08sporting/08sporting-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 585, 'width': 1024, 'subType'