/16yemen-ss-slide-CL1P/16yemen-ss-slide-CL1P-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'From Arizona to Yemen: The Journey of an American Bomb', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Warhead and the Well', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Arms Trade', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civilian Casualties', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Raytheon Company', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mohammed bin Salman (1985- )', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Trump, Donald J', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Yemen', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ARIZONA', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-12-11T10:00:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeffrey E. Stern', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeffrey', 'middlename': 'E.', 'lastname': 'Stern', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4655d37d-05f0-5005-9264-79f3e4e32e35', 'word_count': 7607, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4655d37d-05f0-5005-9264-79f3e4e32e35'}, {'abstract': 'It’s easy to imagine other people are stuck in their insular little worlds. But you’re probably no better at spotting such bubbles than anyone else.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/12/11/magazine/whos-living-in-a-bubble.html', 'snippet': 'It’s easy to imagine other people are stuck in their insular little worlds. But you’re probably no better at spotting such bubbles than anyone else.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There’s a large spot reserved in the popular imagination for the Platonic ideal of an out-of-control market, a stunning collective enthusiasm that was bound to end in ruin: the tulip mania of the Netherlands, which ended in 1637. Tulips, a newly fashionable luxury item, temporarily became more valuable than houses — until, obviously, the inevitable market correction, which ruined fortunes and tore a nation apart.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/16/magazine/16mag-FirstWords-1/16mag-FirstWords-1-articleLarge-v2.png', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/12/16/magazine/16mag-FirstWords-1/16mag-FirstWords-1-articleLarge-v2.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/16/magazine/16mag-FirstWords-1/16mag-FirstWords-1-jumbo-v2.png', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/16/magazine/16mag-FirstWords-1/16mag-FirstWords-1-superJumbo-v2.png', 'height': 2048, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/16/magazine/16mag-FirstWords-1/16mag-FirstWords-1-thumbStandard-v2.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/12/16/magazine/16mag-FirstWords-1/16mag-FirstWords-1-thumbStandard-v2.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/16/magazine/16mag-FirstWords-1/16mag-FirstWords-1-thumbLarge-v2.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Who’s Living in a ‘Bubble’?', 'kicker': 'First Words', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Small Worlds', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bitcoin (Currency)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economics (Theory and Philosophy)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Filter Bubble (Book)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-12-11T10:00:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sasha Chapin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sasha', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chapin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1af433a4-f95c-570d-9a6e-9265f91d4e17', 'word_count': 1476, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1af433a4-f95c-570d-9a6e-9265f91d4e17'}, {'abstract': 'A selection of books published this week; plus, a peek at what our colleagues around the newsroom are reading.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/12/11/books/review/new-noteworthy-jamal-jordan.html', 'snippet': 'A selection of books published this week; plus, a peek at what our colleagues around the newsroom are reading.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PERENNIAL By Kelly Forsythe. (Coffee House, paper, $16.95.) Innocence and violence are the poles of this debut collection about the Columbine school shooting, where even lipstick tubes call to mind gun barrels and where children, like flowers, “are beautiful, then die.” ANAGNORISIS By Kyle Dargan. (Northwestern University, paper, $18.) Dargan’s fifth collection takes its title from the literary term for a moment of sudden recognition. Here, it amounts almost to resignation — about the country’s racist brutality, as in a poem called “Death Toll,” which opens: “This road has surely paid for itself by now, / but let’s toss some more bullets in the bucket.” WHO IS MARY SUE? By Sophie Collins. (Faber & Faber, paper, $16.) Part literary criticism, part feminist manifesto, this debut by an Edinburgh poet uses quotations, aphorisms and verse to explode the archetype of the “Mary Sue,” as she’s known in the world of fan fiction; the closest analog would be the “manic pixie dream girl,” too good to be true. THE GILDED AUCTION BLOCK By Shane McCrae. (Farrar, Straus & Giroux, $23.) This book, McCrae’s sixth, seems a direct response to the 2016 election; many of its poems begin with the word “America” and one, “Everything I Know About Blackness I Learned From Donald Trump,” opens “America I was driving when I heard you / Had died I swerved into a ditch and wept.” SO FAR SO GOOD By Ursula K. Le Guin. (Copper Canyon, $23.) Le Guin died at the beginning of 2018, and this posthumous book is preoccupied with spare musings on age and mortality. Yet her political habit persists: “We don’t understand each other,” she writes, “and ignorance is danger.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/16/books/review/16newnoteworthy0/16newnoteworthy0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/12/16/books/review/16newnoteworthy0/16newnoteworthy0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/16/books/review/16newnoteworthy0/16newnoteworthy0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/16/books/review/16newnoteworthy0/16newnoteworthy0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 814, 'width': 1221, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/16/books/review/16newnoteworthy0/16newnoteworthy0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/12/16/books/review/16newnoteworthy0/16newnoteworthy0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/16/books/review/16newnoteworthy0/16newnoteworthy0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'New & Noteworthy', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-12-11T10:00:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2b8f5207-240d-50b4-99e5-3caa11562583', 'word_count': 492, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2b8f5207-240d-50b4-99e5-3caa11562583'}, {'abstract': 'Cruising can be a pricey way to get away, but these tips will help you book even a high-end cruise and keep enough in your wallet to enjoy your destination.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/12/11/travel/four-ways-to-book-a-luxury-cruise-for-less.html', 'snippet': 'Cruising can be a pricey way to get away, but these tips will help you book even a high-end cruise and keep enough in your wallet to enjoy your destination.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A luxury cruise usually comes with luxury pricing, but there are ways to save money on your next onboard getaway, said Judy Perl, a cruise specialist and the founder of Judy Perl Worldwide Travel. “You can get a lot more for your money if you know what to look for when picking an itinerary,” she said.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/16/travel/16TIPS/merlin_147973191_e6818f1d-43db-45f9-ad7e-5dfa2236c8ee-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/12/16/travel/16TIPS/merlin_147973191_e6818f1d-43db-45f9-ad7e-5dfa2236c8ee-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/16/travel/16TIPS/merlin_147973191_e6818f1d-43db-45f9-ad7e-5dfa2236c8ee-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 735, 'width': 735, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/16/travel/16TIPS/merlin_147973191_e6818f1d-43db-45f9-ad7e-5dfa2236c8ee-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 735, 'width': 735, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/16/travel/16TIPS/merlin_147973191_e6818f1d-43db-45f9-ad7e-5dfa2236c8ee-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/12/16/travel/16TIPS/merlin_147973191_e6818f1d-43db-45f9-ad7e-5dfa2236c8ee-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/16/travel/16TIPS/merlin_147973191_e6818f1d-43db-45f9-ad7e-5dfa2236c8ee-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Four Ways to Book a Luxury Cruise for Less', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Plan to Save On Luxury Cruises', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cruises', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Budget Travel', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-12-11T10:00:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Shivani Vora', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Shivani', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vora', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/05581374-4e38-5c9b-8534-d79a3e7dae23', 'word_count': 559, 'uri': 'nyt://article/05581374-4e38-5c9b-8534-d79a3e7dae23'}, {'abstract': 'The “Marvelous Mrs. Maisel” actor never tires of the City of Light, was fascinated by the Roman ruins in Lebanon and would happily spend the rest of his days in a certain Texas college town.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/12/11/travel/the-travels-of-tony-shalhoub-from-paris-to-austin.html', 'snippet': 'The “Marvelous Mrs. Maisel” actor never tires of the City of Light, was fascinated by the Roman ruins in Lebanon and would happily spend the rest of his days in a certain Texas college town.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It was a trip gone awry that brought Tony Shalhoub his first Tony Award last season, for best performance by a leading actor in a musical. In “The Band’s Visit,” Mr. Shalhoub played the leader of an Arab military band that inadvertently ends up stranded in a small, isolated Israeli town and not the more cultured city it was supposed to travel to for a concert performance. (The fact that one was named Petah Tikva and the other Bet Hatikva — “with a B" — might explain the confusion.)', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/16/travel/16qna-shalhoub2/merlin_147491022_7d981a99-7f3f-4a1a-b6a1-aef323786be3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 900, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/12/16/travel/16qna-shalhoub2/merlin_147491022_7d981a99-7f3f-4a1a-b6a1-aef323786be3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 900}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/16/travel/16qna-shalhoub2/merlin_147491022_7d981a99-7f3f-4a1a-b6a1-aef323786be3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/16/travel/16qna-shalhoub2/merlin_147491022_7d981a99-7f3f-4a1a-b6a1-aef323786be3-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1366, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/16/travel/16qna-shalhoub2/16qna-shalhoub2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/12/16/travel/16qna-shalhoub2/16qna-shalhoub2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/16/travel/16qna-shalhoub2/16qna-shalhoub2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Travels of Tony Shalhoub, From Paris to Austin ', 'kicker': 'Q & A', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Adventures of Tony Shalhoub', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shalhoub, Tony', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ACTORS AND ACTRESSES', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Marvelous Mrs. Maisel (TV Program)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-12-11T10:00:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Ortved', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ortved', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2dd0c211-b0b2-578a-bac1-d95fc5f85280', 'word_count': 866, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2dd0c211-b0b2-578a-bac1-d95fc5f85280'}, {'abstract': 'After a spike in anonymous complaints about store signage, some merchants are facing big fines, and others are taking their signs down as a precaution.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/12/11/nyregion/311-nyc-store-sign-fines.html', 'snippet': 'After a spike in anonymous complaints about store signage, some merchants are facing big fines, and others are taking their signs down as a precaution.', 'lead_paragraph': '[What you need to know to start the day: Get New York Today in your inbox.]', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/12/nyregion/12xp-storesigns1/merlin_147826650_193467f9-885b-496c-9263-e758bae23a7a-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/12/12/nyregion/12xp-storesigns1/merlin_1478