:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rebecca Kelly Slaughter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rebecca', 'middlename': 'Kelly', 'lastname': 'Slaughter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/0768c89b-ab25-536c-b662-e5cc5e369150', 'word_count': 875, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0768c89b-ab25-536c-b662-e5cc5e369150'}, {'abstract': 'Yes, it would be. But that question is a distraction from the principal objective of the Russia investigation.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/12/12/opinion/mueller-indict-trump.html', 'snippet': 'Yes, it would be. But that question is a distraction from the principal objective of the Russia investigation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The government’s recent sentencing memos regarding Michael Cohen, President Trump’s former lawyer, have prompted speculation about whether Mr. Trump himself acted unlawfully during his presidential campaign. The possibility of such criminal conduct has, in turn, renewed an old debate about whether it would be constitutional to indict a sitting United States president.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/12/opinion/12LEDERMAN/merlin_147966483_0664a557-4d66-4c00-9eab-69ad3e2d173f-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/12/12/opinion/12LEDERMAN/merlin_147966483_0664a557-4d66-4c00-9eab-69ad3e2d173f-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/12/opinion/12LEDERMAN/merlin_147966483_0664a557-4d66-4c00-9eab-69ad3e2d173f-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', '