a42ba', 'word_count': 1362, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c3ac87d9-a8ed-5cb8-8962-606f899a42ba'}, {'abstract': 'Make chicken francese, an American-Italian interpretation of French cooking, or abandon recipes altogether and try bananas Foster.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/12/12/dining/the-most-popular-recipes-of-2018.html', 'snippet': 'Make chicken francese, an American-Italian interpretation of French cooking, or abandon recipes altogether and try bananas Foster.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Good morning. We mined our data to come up with a list of the 50 most popular new recipes on NYT Cooking this year. I’m super-stoked to report that the top slot went to Julia Moskin’s recipe for chicken francese (above), an American-Italian interpretation of French cooking that you’ll find on restaurant menus all over the United States but particularly in ones built under the low slate skies of Rochester, N.Y., where the dish is known as “chicken French.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/12/dining/12chickenFRalt/12chickenFRalt-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/12/12/dining/12chickenFRalt/12chickenFRalt-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/12/dining/12chickenFRalt/12chickenFRalt-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/12/dining/12chickenFRalt/12chickenFRalt-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/201