election, saying that The National Enquirer’s parent company was cooperating.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/12/13/us/politics/on-politics-trump-national-enquirer.html', 'snippet': 'Prosecutors took a big step in their inquiry into payouts made to two women ahead of the 2016 election, saying that The National Enquirer’s parent company was cooperating.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Good Thursday morning. Here are some of the stories making news in Washington and politics today.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'On Politics: Tabloid Company Agrees to Cooperate in Trump Hush-Money Inquiry', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Morning Edition', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-12-13T09:30:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Politics', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2c28f7ab-2182-5b96-af0e-0e34d92b1a4e', 'word_count': 585, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2c28f7ab-2182-5b96-af0e-0e34d92b1a4e'}, {'abstract': 'She was in therapy for a death in her family. Most of her doctors thought her vision problem was stress related — except one.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/12/13/magazine/why-was-the-woman-having-trouble-seeing-if-her-eyes-were-fine.html', 'snippet': 'She was in therapy for a death in her family. Most of her doctors thought her vision problem was stress related — except one.', 'lead_paragraph': '“You look awful,” Esther Sarid, a psychiatrist in Toronto, told her patient. She’d been seeing the 46-year-old woman for nearly a year, after the sudden death of her brother. Despite her grief, the patient continued to work hard as a professor at a Canadian university. But Sarid knew it had been a tough time for her.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/16/magazine/16mag-Diagnosis-1/16mag-Diagnosis-1-articleLarge.png', 'height': 551, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/12/16/magazine/16mag-Diagnosis-1/16mag-Diagnosis-1-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 551}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/16/magazine/16mag-Diagnosis-1/16mag-Diagnosis-1-jumbo.png', 'height': 940, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/16/magazine/16mag-Diagnosis-1/16mag-Diagnosis-1-superJumbo.png', 'height': 1541, 'width': 1678, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/16/magazine/16mag-Diagnosis-1/16mag-Diagnosis-1-thumbStandard.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/12/16/magazine/16mag-Diagnosis-1/16mag-Diagnosis-1-thumbStandard.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/16/magazine/16mag-Diagnosis-1/16mag-Diagnosis-1-thumbLarge.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Why Was the Woman Having Trouble Seeing, if Her Eyes Were Fine?', 'kicker': 'Diagnosis', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Why Was the Woman Having Trouble Seeing, if Her Eyes Were Fine?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Eyes and Eyesight', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Immune System', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nerves and Nervous System', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-12-13T10:00:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lisa Sanders, M.D', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lisa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sanders', 'qualifier': 'M.D', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/548ae96c-315d-5c30-a26f-7308dc1ba6fd', 'word_count': 1378, 'uri': 'nyt://article/548ae96c-315d-5c30-a26f-7308dc1ba6fd'}, {'abstract': 'The actor on his “double act” movies and what acting and the priesthood have in common.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/12/13/magazine/john-c-reilly-wants-to-play-vulnerable-men.html', 'snippet': 'The actor on his “double act” movies and what acting and the priesthood have in common.', 'lead_paragraph': 'You have four “double act” movies coming out around the same time: “Stan & Ollie,” “Holmes & Watson,” “The Sisters Brothers” and “Ralph Breaks the Internet.” Do you consciously seek out work about duos? I’ve done a lot of stuff where I was the best friend — whether it’s with Will Ferrell in “Step Brothers” or Mark Wahlberg in “Boogie Nights.” That’s a recurring thing in my life too: From the time I was a little kid, I always had a best friend, and I would really heavily invest in single friendships. One of those truly magical things that human beings can do together is to create harmony, to create a third thing that wasn’t there before the two decided to cooperate. So I try to do work that’s bigger than myself. I didn’t become an actor because I have a big ego — I did it because I love collaborating with people. I find it really lonely to work alone, and the pressure is just too great when you have to come up with every idea yourself.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '66', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/16/magazine/16mag-talk-image2/16mag-talk-image2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/12/16/magazine/16mag-talk-image2/16mag-talk-image2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/16/magazine/16mag-talk-image2/16mag-talk-image2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/16/magazine/16mag-talk-image2/16mag-talk-image2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/16/magazine/16mag-talk-image2/16mag-talk-image2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/12/16/magazine/16mag-talk-image2/16mag-talk-image2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/16/magazine/16mag-talk-image2/16mag-talk-image2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'John C. Reilly Wants to Play Vulnerable Men', 'kicker': 'Talk', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'John C. Reilly Wants to Play Vulnerable Men', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ACTORS AND ACTRESSES', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Reilly, John C', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-12-13T10:00:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'Interview by Molly Lambert', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Interview', 'middlename': 'Molly', 'lastname': 'Lambert', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b7cbeb5c-8cb4-5676-b2c2-dac321c0f511', 'word_count': 706, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b7cbeb5c-8cb4-5676-b2c2-dac321c0f511'}, {'abstract': 'The director’s new film tells the story of his childhood in Mexico — but seen from the vantage of the domestic worker who raised him.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/12/13/magazine/alfonso-cuaron-roma-mexico-netflix.html', 'snippet': 'The director’s new film tells the story of his childhood in Mexico — but seen from the vantage of the domestic worker who raised him.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In Pietrasanta, Italy — a small town where Michelangelo used to shop for marble — a tall brick bell tower tolls throughout the day from a church on one side of the historic central square. Several enormous sculptures punctuate the plaza: faceless heads with bizarre hair wrought in brass, iron and steel. Against the green hills of Tuscany and the imposing white marble church, they look like the remains of a Dadaist prank. Around the plaza’s edges, tourists and locals congregate in cafes for morning cappuccini and evening aperitivi. This fall, the 57-year-old Mexican film director Alfonso Cuarón, whose youngest child attends school in Pietrasanta, met me there for endless cups of green tea. At a distance, walking through the square in a hoodie and black sneakers, cellphone pressed against his head, Cuarón looked like a middle-aged skater. Up close, speaking in Spanish, the language in which he still dreams and in which we held all our conversations, he often regarded me with a shrewd gaze. But then, once, Cuarón broke off midsentence to point through the plaza.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '49', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/16/magazine/16mag-cuaron-slide-L77I/16mag-cuaron-slide-L77I-articleLarge.png', 'height': 759, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/12/16/magazine/16mag-cuaron-slide-L77I/16mag-cuaron-slide-L77I-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 759}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/16/magazine/16mag-cuaron-slide-L77I/16mag-cuaron-slide-L77I-jumbo.png', 'height': 1024, 'width': 810, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/16/magazine/16mag-cuaron-slide-L77I/16mag-cuaron-slide-L77I-superJumbo.png', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1619, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/16/magazine/16mag-cuaron-slide-L77I/16mag-cuaron-slide-L77I-thumbStandard.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/12/16/magazine/16mag-cuaron-slide-L77I/16mag-cuaron-slide-L77I-thumbStandard.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/16/magazine/16mag-cuaron-slide-L77I/16mag-cuaron-slide-L77I-thumbLarge.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'After ‘Gravity,’ Alfonso Cuarón Had His Pick of Directing Blockbusters. Instead, He Went Home to Make ‘Roma.’', 'kicker': 'Feature', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Revisionary', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cuaron, Alfonso', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mexico City (Mexico)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Roma (Movie)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-12-13T10:00:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marcela Valdes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marcela', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Valdes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0223fada-8a5b-5d3d-837f-663f5279bef8', 'word_count': 6137, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0223fada-8a5b-5d3d-837f-663f5279bef8'}, {'abstract': 'Can a writer today really channel the voice of P.G. Wodehouse?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/12/13/magazine/new-sentences-from-jeeves-and-the-king-of-clubs.html', 'snippet': 'Can a writer today really channel the voice of P.G. Wodehouse?', 'lead_paragraph': '— From “Jeeves and the King of Clubs” (Little, Brown & Company, 2018, Page 9), an homage to the classic P.G. Wodehouse characters by the author Ben Schott.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/16/magazine/16mag-sentences1/16mag-sentences1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 791, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/12/16/magazine/16mag-sentences1/16mag-sentences1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 791}}, {'rank':