alue': 'Hospitals', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Heart', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-12-13T10:00:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Well', 'section_name': 'Well', 'subsection_name': 'Live', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicholas Bakalar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicholas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bakalar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0702fe05-624d-593b-a358-0e35108534d3', 'word_count': 227, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0702fe05-624d-593b-a358-0e35108534d3'}, {'abstract': 'More than eight hours long and harrowing to watch, Wang Bing’s “Dead Souls” is a necessary look at China’s brutal re-education camps for “rightists.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/12/13/movies/dead-souls-review.html', 'snippet': 'More than eight hours long and harrowing to watch, Wang Bing’s “Dead Souls” is a necessary look at China’s brutal re-education camps for “rightists.”', 'lead_paragraph': '“Nobody wants to talk about it. And most are dead.” That’s how one subject describes the legacy of the re-education camps that supposed “rightists” were sent to in the 1950s and ’60s in Mao Zedong’s China. Filmed over more than a decade, the director Wang Bing’s monumental, more than eight-hour documentary, “Dead Souls,” seeks to fill an important part of the historical record through extended interviews with about a dozen aging survivors of Jiabiangou, a complex of three work camps in northwest China where conditions were particularly dire.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/14/arts/14DEADSOULS/merlin_147854748_d387f33f-1ce1-407b-8228-45241f68883e-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge'