own’s photographs of Las Vegas during its early, explosive years of growth were meant to aid rethinking about architecture: how grandiose signage and bright neon lights eclipsed design, particularly in the age of the automobile. These ideas were explained — and also embraced — in “Learning From Las Vegas” (1972), which Ms. Scott Brown wrote with her husband and design partner, Robert Venturi, and their architectural colleague Steven Izenour, and are evident in Ms. Scott Brown’s “Photographs, 1956-1966,” at Carriage Trade.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/14/arts/14galleries-brown/merlin_147616770_f4be5cef-42f4-4c7c-99a1-e5dbc0ee261c-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 398, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/12/14/arts/14galleries-brown/merlin_147616770_f4be5cef-42f4-4c7c-99a1-e5dbc0ee261c-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 398}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/14/arts/14galleries-brown/merlin_147616770_f4be5cef-42f4-4c7c-99a1-e5dbc0ee261c-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 679, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/14/arts/14galleries-brown/merlin_147616770_f4be5cef-42f4-4c7c-99a1-e5dbc0ee261c-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1358, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/14/arts/14galleries-brown/14galleries-brown-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/12/14/arts/14galleries-brown/14galleri