', 'value': 'Hospitals', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Delhi (India)', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-12-15T19:30:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sohini Chattopadhyay', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sohini', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chattopadhyay', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/152bfc1e-9a63-530a-ac4a-d9be5fcda045', 'word_count': 1033, 'uri': 'nyt://article/152bfc1e-9a63-530a-ac4a-d9be5fcda045'}, {'abstract': 'Officials in several countries are getting the message: Cities are about people, not cars. ', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/12/15/opinion/sunday/cars-pedestrians-cities.html', 'snippet': 'Officials in several countries are getting the message: Cities are about people, not cars. ', 'lead_paragraph': 'In many of the major cities of the world, it has begun to dawn even on public officials that walking is a highly efficient means of transit, as well as one of the great underrated pleasures in life. A few major cities have even tentatively begun to take back their streets for pedestrians.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/16/opinion/16conniff02/16conniff02-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/12/16/opinion/16conniff02/16conniff02-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'ima