ience/saturn-rings-vanish.html', 'snippet': 'The “ring rain” that falls into the gas giant is so abundant that the icy bands could disappear in 300 million years, or even sooner.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Saturn’s icy rings are among the most iconic features in the solar system. But they’re raining so much water onto the planet that in 300 million years they could rain themselves nearly out of existence, leaving Saturn startlingly ringless.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/18/science/18TB-SATURN1/18TB-SATURN1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/12/18/science/18TB-SATURN1/18TB-SATURN1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/18/science/18TB-SATURN1/18TB-SATURN1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/18/science/18TB-SATURN1/18TB-SATURN1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/18/science/18TB-SATURN1/18TB-SATURN1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/12/18/science/18TB-SATURN1/18TB-SATURN1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/18/science/18TB-SATURN1/18TB-SATURN1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLa