heir Special Immigrant Visa application. Those who do get approved face a whole new set of problems once they arrive in the United States.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Strolling the aisles at a Kohl’s department store near his home in Rochester, N.Y., Azizullah Sharifi spoke Pashto with his daughter Marwah as they picked through shorts, T-shirts and flip-flops for the summer. The father and daughter stopped to check their shopping cart, when a woman next to them muttered, “Speak [expletive] English” in a low growl. Once Sharifi realized she was talking to him, he quickly pushed his cart away without responding. But he wasn’t fast enough. Though she was only 7, Marwah recognized that the woman had “said something really bad.” “Just ignore her,” Sharifi told her. It was a drastic shift from the way he was treated in his home country of Afghanistan, where American service members, with whom he had worked closely, treated him with respect. Much of his experience in the United States has been positive, but sometimes, “you feel Islamophobia, the racism — not all people, but you can feel it,” Sharifi said.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/18/magazine/18ATWAR-afghanvisas-image1/18ATWAR-afghanvisas-image1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/12/18/magazine/18ATWAR-afghanvisas-image1/18ATWAR-afghanvisas-image1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/18/magazine/18ATWAR-afghanvisas-image1/18ATWAR-afghanvisas-image1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/18/magazine/18ATWAR-afghanvisas-image1/18ATWAR-afghanvisas-image1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/18/magazine/18ATWAR-afghanvisas-image1/18ATWAR-afghanvisas-image1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/12/18/magazine/18ATWAR-afghanvisas-image1/18ATWAR-afghanvisas-image1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/18/magazine/18ATWAR-afghanvisas-image1/18ATWAR-afghanvisas-image1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In the United States, His Problem Wasn’t the Taliban. It Was Everything Else.', 'kicker': 'At War', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Afghanistan War (2001- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Immigration and Emigration', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Translation and Interpreters', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'State Department', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFGHANISTAN', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-12-18T10:00:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kenneth R. Rosen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kenneth', 'middlename': 'R.', 'lastname': 'Rosen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/40dd3887-be27-5ff2-bff4-1a3b15cc24c8', 'word_count': 2178, 'uri': 'nyt://article/40dd3887-be27-5ff2-bff4-1a3b15cc24c8'}, {'abstract': 'Two persons needed: one to command the animal to slide onto a platform and keep still, the other to dry the mammary region and work the pump.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/12/18/magazine/how-to-milk-a-killer-whale.html', 'snippet': 'Two persons needed: one to command the animal to slide onto a platform and keep still, the other to dry the mammary region and work the pump.', 'lead_paragraph': '“You need to have a relationship with the whale,” says Dawn Noren, a research fishery biologist for the National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration. It would be hazardous to try milking any cetacean in the wild (although scientists have done so with recently deceased specimens). In captivity, though, the animals can be trained to tolerate it. Noren recently conducted a study that required 15 months of regularly milking two killer whale mothers at SeaWorld. She is using those milk samples to better understand how industrial toxins like polychlorinated biphenyls are passed in milk from mothers to calves among a different, endangered subgroup of the species: the so-called Southern Resident killer whales of the Pacific Northwest.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/23/magazine/23Mag-Tip-1/23Mag-Tip-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/12/23/magazine/23Mag-Tip-1/23Mag-Tip-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/23/magazine/23Mag-Tip-1/23Mag-Tip-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/23/magazine/23Mag-Tip-1/23Mag-Tip-1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1493, 'width': 1493, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/23/magazine/23Mag-Tip-1/23Mag-Tip-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/12/23/magazine/23Mag-Tip-1/23Mag-Tip-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/23/magazine/23Mag-Tip-1/23Mag-Tip-1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How to Milk a Killer Whale', 'kicker': 'Tip', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'How to Milk a Killer Whale', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Whales and Whaling', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fish and Other Marine Life', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'SeaWorld Entertainment Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pacific Northwestern States (US)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-12-18T10:00:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Malia Wollan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Malia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wollan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5aa31d21-1f35-5b18-8da4-2a0f6ee230ca', 'word_count': 379, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5aa31d21-1f35-5b18-8da4-2a0f6ee230ca'}, {'abstract': 'The big online dating rebrand means the apps want you to fall in love with them now.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/12/18/style/dating-apps-tinder-bumble-content.html', 'snippet': 'The big online dating rebrand means the apps want you to fall in love with them now.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Tinder and Bumble are desperate to convince you that you’re not desperate. Dating, they promise, is fun, so fun, that when one date ends badly, it’s a barely disguised blessing: You get to stay on the apps and keep on dating! ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/23/style/23swipelife-woman/23swipelife-woman-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 386, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/12/23/style/23swipelife-woman/23swipelife-woman-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 386}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/23/style/23swipelife-woman/23swipelife-woman-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 659, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/23/style/23swipelife-woman/23swipelife-woman-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1319, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/23/style/23swipelife-woman/23swipelife-woman-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/12/23/style/23swipelife-woman/23swipelife-woman-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/23/style/23swipelife-woman/23swipelife-woman-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Tinder and Bumble Are Hungry for Your Love', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Dating Apps Want Us to Settle Down (With Them)', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Online Dating', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bumble Trading Inc', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Tinder (Mobile App)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Grindr.com', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-12-18T10:00:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jonah Engel Bromwich', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jonah', 'middlename': 'Engel', 'lastname': 'Bromwich', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/759baff9-f224-52cf-9b1e-c59a2526266a', 'word_count': 1761, 'uri': 'nyt://article/759baff9-f224-52cf-9b1e-c59a2526266a'}, {'abstract': 'Conflicts, corruption, Russian influence and politicizing government are just some of the areas of inquiry they have proposed.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/12/18/magazine/trump-investigation-democrats-russia.html', 'snippet': 'Conflicts, corruption, Russian influence and politicizing government are just some of the areas of inquiry they have proposed.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In two weeks, congressional Democrats will return to Washington with the authority to investigate a White House that is suspected of foreign collusion, conflicts of interest and mismanagement of the federal government.  Jason Zengerle wrote an article about three Democratic representatives who will become chairmen of committees with powerful investigative mandates. These are some potential areas of inquiry proposed in 2017 and 2018 by House Democrats on those committees:', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/23/magazine/23mag-trump-sidebar6/23mag-trump-sidebar6-articleLarge-v6.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/12/23/magazine/23mag-trump-sidebar6/23mag-trump-sidebar6-articleLarge-v6.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/23/magazine/23mag-trump-sidebar6/23mag-trump-sidebar6-jumbo-v6.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/23/magazine/23mag-trump-sidebar6/23mag-trump-sidebar6-superJumbo-v6.jpg', 'height': 1935, 'width': 1935, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/23/magazine/23mag-trump-sidebar6/23mag-trump-sidebar6-thumbStandard-v6.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/12/23/magazine/23mag-trump-sidebar6/23mag-trump-sidebar6-thumbStandard-v6.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/23/magazine/23mag-trump-sidebar6/23mag-trump-sidebar6-thumbLarge-v6.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In an Administration Full of Investigative Targets, Where Would the Democrats Start?', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Government Operations', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Russian Interference in 2016 US Elections and Ties to Trump Associates', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Democratic Party', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Conflicts of Interest', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-12-18T10:00:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alex Carp', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alex', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carp', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0831c365-b382-512c-a5fd-10ce94bb8d36', 'word_count': 787, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0831c365-b382-512c-a5fd-10ce94bb8d36'}, {'abstract': 'The magazine’s Ethicist columnist on whether the children of parents who could earn higher salaries should qualify for financial aid and more.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/12/18/magazine/should-kids-whose-parents-could-earn-higher-salaries-get-financial-aid.html', 'snippet': 'The magazine’s Ethicist columnist on whether the children of parents who could earn higher salaries should qualify for financial aid and more.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I have a friend with school-age children who is a professional in a fairly lucrative field. The spouse is in a nonlucrative field. My friend chose to work fewer hours to spend time with their growing children, greatly reducing the family’s take-home pay in the process.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'pr