e power to investigate the White House.', 'lead_paragraph': '', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Behind the Cover: Trump’s Inquisitors', 'kicker': 'Issue 12.23.18', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Design', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-12-19T18:00:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/acd94dd6-ad74-57c6-828d-c798acce9c4f', 'word_count': 84, 'uri': 'nyt://article/acd94dd6-ad74-57c6-828d-c798acce9c4f'}, {'abstract': 'In “The Word Pretty,” Elisa Gabbert delivers personal essays that are more inquisitive and obsessive than most.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/12/19/books/review-word-pretty-elisa-gabbert.html', 'snippet': 'In “The Word Pretty,” Elisa Gabbert delivers personal essays that are more inquisitive and obsessive than most.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Perhaps no imperative cliché is disobeyed more often than “Don’t judge a book by its cover.” And with good cause; you can learn a decent amount about a book this way. Take Elisa Gabbert’s “The Word Pretty.” Its deep blue cover, with its elegantly minimalist font and picture of a woman ambiguously hiding her face with her hands — is she crying or laughing? ashamed? repulsed? — manages to be both unassuming and striking, austere and inviting.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/20/books/20bookgabbert1/19bookgabbert1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 935, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/12/20/books/20bookgabbert1/19bookgabbert1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 9