ning', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d01c0a6b-cd30-5fd4-9176-83c5b3523af6', 'word_count': 18, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d01c0a6b-cd30-5fd4-9176-83c5b3523af6'}, {'abstract': 'Some feedback from your fiction professor.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/12/22/opinion/sunday/humor-rudolph-creative-writing.html', 'snippet': 'Some feedback from your fiction professor.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Your opening could be much stronger. You start by listing a bunch of characters the reader is somehow supposed to “know,” but their names are things like “Vixen and Blitzen” and “Dancer and Prancer.” Was the rhyming intentional? It was distracting. Remember that even though it’s fiction, it’s meant to be believable. Give these characters names like Fred, Luke, Greg, etc.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/22/opinion/22LOOSE/22LOOSE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 753, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/12/22/opinion/22LOOSE/22LOOSE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 753}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/22/opinion/22LOOSE/22LOOSE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 816, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/22/opinion/22LOOSE/22LOOSE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1631, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/22/opinion/22LOOSE/22LOOSE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', '