ole': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/908526e8-9c22-5955-9bfa-39a24b2933a3', 'word_count': 1248, 'uri': 'nyt://article/908526e8-9c22-5955-9bfa-39a24b2933a3'}, {'abstract': 'Jewish families and others used to flock to Chinese restaurants because that was all that was open, but now it’s almost as traditional as milk and cookies for Santa.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/12/25/opinion/christmas-chinese-food.html', 'snippet': 'Jewish families and others used to flock to Chinese restaurants because that was all that was open, but now it’s almost as traditional as milk and cookies for Santa.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When my mother first came to America over three decades ago, she waited tables for five years at a restaurant called Forbidden City in Ann Arbor, Mich. She quit when she became pregnant with me, but I grew up hearing stories about her time as a waitress. Stories about stingy customers and cooks she tried to avoid — and the busiest day of the year: Christmas.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/25/opinion/25liWeb/merlin_147956427_1bf3a1f2-0396-4029-817f-8a931c21a66b-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/12/25/opinion/25liWeb/merlin_147956427_1bf3a1f2-0396-4029-817f-8a931c21a66b-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/25/opinion/25liWeb/merlin_147956427_1bf3a1f2-0396-4029-817f-8a931c21a66b-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/25/opinion/25liWeb/merlin_147956427_1bf3a1f