by wind.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The final miles of a nearly two-month race across Antarctica — a lonely effort marked by long days, short nights and stunning endurance — ended Wednesday with a sprint to the finish.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/27/sports/27-ANTARCTICA-03-onlinesub/merlin_148505520_3f628fab-3492-420d-8648-e72e56dbe05b-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 525, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/12/27/sports/27-ANTARCTICA-03-onlinesub/merlin_148505520_3f628fab-3492-420d-8648-e72e56dbe05b-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 525}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/27/sports/27-ANTARCTICA-03-onlinesub/merlin_148505520_3f628fab-3492-420d-8648-e72e56dbe05b-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 656, 'width': 750, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/27/sports/27-ANTARCTICA-03-onlinesub/merlin_148505520_3f628fab-3492-420d-8648-e72e56dbe05b-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 656, 'width': 750, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/27/sports/27-ANTARCTICA-03-onlinesub/27antarctica-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/12/27/sports/27-ANTARCTICA-03-onlinesub/27antarctica-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/27/sports/27-ANTARCTICA-03-onlinesub/27antarctica-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Colin O’Brady Completes Crossing of Antarctica With Final 32-Hour Push', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Antarctica Trek Is Completed, And Every Icy Stride Is Savored', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ANTARCTIC REGIONS', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': "O'Brady, Colin (1985- )", 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rudd, Louis', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Skiing', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-12-26T18:08:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam Skolnick', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Skolnick', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c88f82ff-edb1-56ec-a067-3b9070b9e39f', 'word_count': 1290, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c88f82ff-edb1-56ec-a067-3b9070b9e39f'}, {'abstract': 'A. S. Hamrah, the resident movie critic of the journal n+1, is committed to his ambivalence, conveying it with a mixture of precision and conviction.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/12/26/books/review-earth-dies-streaming-film-writing-a-s-hamrah.html', 'snippet': 'A. S. Hamrah, the resident movie critic of the journal n+1, is committed to his ambivalence, conveying it with a mixture of precision and conviction.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It can be hard for criticism to cohere when it’s perforated by ambivalence. A rave coasts along, buoyed by enthusiasm. A pan serves up the dramatic tension on a platter: The people trying to sell you a book (or movie or play) insist that it’s great, and here’s why it’s unremittingly awful. Mixed feelings are trickier, and more vulnerable. They’re like unstable elements waiting to be pulled into a wishy-washy middle ground.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/27/books/27bookhamrah1/27bookhamrah1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 936, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/12/27/books/27bookhamrah1/27bookhamrah1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 936}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/27/books/27bookhamrah1/27bookhamrah1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 657, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/27/books/27bookhamrah1/27bookhamrah1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1838, 'width': 1178, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/27/books/27bookhamrah1/27bookhamrah1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/12/27/books/27bookhamrah1/27bookhamrah1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/27/books/27bookhamrah1/27bookhamrah1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In ‘The Earth Dies Streaming,’ Film Criticism That Transcends Raves and Pans', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Don’t Blurb Me on That', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Earth Dies Streaming (Book)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hamrah, A S', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-12-26T18:19:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Szalai', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Szalai', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/2887285c-dcc1-5525-8737-8ee9e16deeca', 'word_count': 1065, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2887285c-dcc1-5525-8737-8ee9e16deeca'}, {'abstract': 'Rome’s puny tree last year was the sorriest of holiday sights. But this year’s lush, lavishly decorated thing is much worse.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/12/26/opinion/rome-christmas-tree.html', 'snippet': 'Rome’s puny tree last year was the sorriest of holiday sights. But this year’s lush, lavishly decorated thing is much worse.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ROME — At the end of 2017, Italy produced the most unlikely of antiheroes: Spelacchio, or Mangy, an ill-fated Christmas tree that arrived for display at Piazza Venezia in Rome with its needles falling off. It was the sorriest of holiday sights, and self-deprecating Romans developed an ironic but heartfelt affection for it. Spelacchio was soon decked out in handwritten notes of affection and support, and with a social media persona that gave it a human voice: its own Twitter account, @spelacchio, in the Roman dialect; several Facebook profiles and an official page on Instagram. #Spelacchio became a popular hashtag.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/26/opinion/26Sala/26Sala-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 397, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/12/26/opinion/26Sala/26Sala-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 397}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/26/opinion/26Sala/26Sala-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 677, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/26/opinion/26Sala/26Sala-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1354, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/26/opinion/26Sala/26Sala-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/12/26/opinion/26Sala/26Sala-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/26/opinion/26Sala/26Sala-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'What Does an Official Christmas Tree Say About a Country?', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'I Miss Rome’s Shabby Tree', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Christmas', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Christmas Trees', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Media', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Netflix Inc', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Raggi, Virginia (1978- )', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Salvini, Matteo', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Rome (Italy)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Italy', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-12-26T18:23:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ilaria Maria Sala', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ilaria', 'middlename': 'Maria', 'lastname': 'Sala', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/ebf8ceb4-2019-54da-b84d-99216070819b', 'word_count': 839, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ebf8ceb4-2019-54da-b84d-99216070819b'}, {'abstract': 'Officers quietly wield a lot of unchecked prosecutorial power.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/12/26/opinion/police-prosecutors-misdemeanors.html', 'snippet': 'Officers quietly wield a lot of unchecked prosecutorial power.', 'lead_paragraph': 'American voters have realized that prosecutors hold the keys to a fairer criminal justice system. In November’s elections, they replaced numerous incumbents with reformers who promised to reduce mass incarceration, its exorbitant costs and its racial disparities. Prosecutors are central to solving these problems because they control two of the most important decisions in the criminal process: who will be charged with a crime and what that criminal charge will be. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/26/opinion/26Natapoff/26Natapoff-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/12/26/opinion/26Natapoff/26Natapoff-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/26/opinion/26Natapoff/2