, 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Afghanistan War (2001- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Taliban', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Afghan National Security Forces', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFGHANISTAN', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-12-27T18:59:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Fatima Faizi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Fatima', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Faizi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5163fbf3-ea28-55b9-a689-f34b62f87e1d', 'word_count': 647, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5163fbf3-ea28-55b9-a689-f34b62f87e1d'}, {'abstract': 'In her column, Hillary Chute reviews four new graphic novels that use the form to challenge societal expectations about sex.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/12/27/books/review/dirty-plotte-julie-doucet.html', 'snippet': 'In her column, Hillary Chute reviews four new graphic novels that use the form to challenge societal expectations about sex.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In Julie Doucet’s comics, body parts are often changing: growing, detaching, breaking, leaking, morphing. On the letters page of an issue of her comic book “Dirty Plotte,” which ran from 1987 to 1998, one reader wrote in, “Stay cool and don’t be sad if you run out of bodily functions.” He need hardly have worried. Doucet, a Montreal-based French Canadian artist, is known for her influential 1999 graphic autobiography, “My New York Diary,” the chronicle of a very bad relationship, but the seething, exuberant comics world she creates is not only tethered to reality. It includes all sorts of fanciful, surrealistic stories with invented or hoped-for bodily functions, like one in which men insert tampons into their urethras, or “The Double,” in which the Julie protagonist happily turns into a man, only to later encounter the prior female self as a separate body. The two Julies then pleasurably have intercourse. Doucet brilliantly toggles between charting dailiness and fantasy.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/30/books/review/30Chute-01/merlin_148144230_4921acb0-659b-438b-8bd9-98c3d10765e4-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 814, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/12/30/books/review/30Chute-01/merlin_148144230_4921acb0-659b-438b-8bd9-98c3d10765e4-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 814}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/30/books/review/30Chute-01/merlin_148144230_4921acb0-659b-438b-8bd9-98c3d10765e4-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 755, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/30/books/review/30Chute-01/merlin_148144230_4921acb0-659b-438b-8bd9-98c3d10765e4-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1510, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/30/books/review/30Chute-01/30Chute-01-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/12/30/books/review/30Chute-01/30Chute-01-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/30/books/review/30Chute-01/30Chute-01-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'th