tml', 'snippet': 'Sex, foreign intrigue, faith, the corruption of ideas, and man-eating tigers, oh my — more great reading for edification and pleasure.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In her essay “Why Are Young People Having So Little Sex?” Kate Julian takes a question that seems to have a simple answer (porn) and shows that it has a complex answer. In one striking part of the essay, which appeared in The Atlantic, Julian shows that fewer young people are having the kind of relationships that lead to sex. In 1995, 74 percent of 17-year-old women had had a special romantic relationship in the preceding 18 months. By 2014, only 46 percent of 17-year-olds had ever had a romantic relationship of any kind.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/27/opinion/27brooksWeb/merlin_148479171_4948c2ad-c58d-498d-a1df-f02c00823450-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 730, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/12/27/opinion/27brooksWeb/merlin_148479171_4948c2ad-c58d-498d-a1df-f02c00823450-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 730}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/27/opinion/27brooksWeb/merlin_148479171_4948c2ad-c58d-498d-a1df-f02c00823450-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 843, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/27/opinion/27brooksWeb/merlin_148479171_4948c2ad-c58d-498d-a1df-f02c00823450-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1685, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/27/opinion/27brooksWeb/27brooksWeb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'he