None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2018-12-28T23:00:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'NYTNow', 'section_name': 'Briefing', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jean Rutter and David Scull', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jean', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rutter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Scull', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'briefing', '_id': 'nyt://article/492551ed-8ba9-5eb6-8911-ff55435f3452', 'word_count': 1254, 'uri': 'nyt://article/492551ed-8ba9-5eb6-8911-ff55435f3452'}, {'abstract': 'We might be able to save honeybees from viruses transmitted by invasive parasites without chemical treatment.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/12/28/opinion/bees-threats-crop-loss-mushrooms.html', 'snippet': 'We might be able to save honeybees from viruses transmitted by invasive parasites without chemical treatment.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Sometime in the 1980s, microscopic mites that had been afflicting honeybees outside the United States found their way to Florida and Wisconsin and began wreaking havoc across the country. These parasites have invaded and decimated wild and domestic bee colonies. Along with other dangers facing bees, like pesticides and the loss of forage lands, the viruses these mites carry threaten the bees we rely on to pollinate many of the fruits, nuts and vegetables we eat. ', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/12/30/opinion/sunday/30stamets-hope/30stamets-hope-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 464, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/12/30/opinion/sunday/30stamets-hope/30stamets-hope-artic